  <Section eyebrow="Limitations" title="What went wrong, and what it means" lede="Honest accounting from the hardware report, so the next revision does not repeat it.">
    <div class="grid grid-2">
      <Callout kind="warn" title="U10 does not work">The string-potential INA239 sits behind an ISO7762 that was wired backwards. Removing the isolator did not revive the sensor, so the top module voltage comes from the electronic load through the Pi. This also limited testing to lower-output modules. <a href={href('/handover/')}>Handover</a> has the workaround.</Callout>
      <Callout kind="warn" title="Heatsink shenanigans">The heatsink is clamped under the board by printed keepers with thermal paste; the first fit needed trimming and the paste was reapplied after the rework. Inductor temperature was only ever estimated by touch. The INA239 die temperatures stayed in the 30–50 °C range on both test days.</Callout>
      <Callout kind="warn" title="Coupled inductor, second-guessed">The winding arrangement was reworked during bring-up. The control model only agrees with the hardware for the final arrangement (circulating mode sees L+M), so any inductor change must be checked with an open-loop probe before closing the loop.</Callout>
      <Callout kind="warn" title="Current telemetry">The ADC gain was set for 100 mV/A while one fitted sensor output 25 mV/A, and in the PV test one leg of shuffler 1 read wrongly for a reason never found. Verify every channel against a direct measurement.</Callout>
      <Callout kind="info" title="Fixed string voltage, no MPPT">Every comparison was made at a fixed 60–70 V. The true gain over a tracked string was never measured, and fixed losses (ripple, gate drive, dead time) dominate at light load: 93.8 % of the supply reached the load, but the shuffler's own efficiency on the 15 W it processed was about 70 %.</Callout>
      <Callout kind="info" title="Fixed dead time">Two 200 ns dead times are 8 % of the period at 200 kHz and 16 % at 400 kHz, which distorts the effective duty: module-voltage spread 0.2 V to 1.1 V, output down. Adaptive dead time is first on the list.</Callout>
    </div>
